**Compile-time vs runtime:** overloading is resolved by parameter types at compile time; overriding is resolved by the actual object type at runtime — interviewers expect you to distinguish both.

```java
// Coupled — every caller breaks when you swap
ArrayList<String> names = new ArrayList<>();

// Abstracted — callers depend on the List contract only
List<String> names = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> names = new LinkedList<>();            // swap: zero callers affected
List<String> names = Collections.unmodifiableList(src); // or this
```

The same principle in your own code: `UserRepository` as an interface means `UserService` never changes when you swap `PostgresUserRepository` for `FakeUserRepository` in tests.

