        if (min > max) throw new IllegalArgumentException("min > max");
    }
}
```

You can override `equals()`, `hashCode()`, or `toString()`, but doing so carries risk. If you exclude a component from `equals()`, two records with different field values could be considered equal — breaking the **semantic transparency** that records guarantee. The compiler won't stop you, but tools and frameworks that rely on records being value-based will behave unexpectedly.

Accessors can be overridden to return **defensive copies**. If a record holds a mutable object like `List`, returning the original reference lets callers mutate it. Overriding `items()` to return `Collections.unmodifiableList(items)` preserves immutability — though the better practice is storing an unmodifiable copy in the compact constructor.

